        <h2 id="dk-doorstep" class="dk-h2">On the doorstep by 5:30</h2>
        <p class="dk-side">Pick a time. The paper builds every morning and goes wherever you read.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="dk-prose">
        <p>
          A <strong>Kindle</strong> gets today’s edition over USB the moment it is plugged in,
          or by email over Wi-Fi through your own mail account. A <strong>Kobo</strong> gets a
          KEPUB and a <strong>PocketBook</strong> an EPUB, in a Deckle folder, the first time
          one is connected. A <strong>reMarkable</strong> gets a PDF laid out for its screen,
          crossword and all, over the USB cable or over SSH and Wi-Fi into a Deckle folder that
          Deckle keeps tidy. Anything running <strong>KOReader</strong> takes it over Wi-Fi from
          Deckle’s own catalog, or lets the Deckle plugin fetch it when the reader wakes. An
          <strong>export folder</strong> in iCloud Drive or Dropbox puts it on an iPad or a
          Boox. Only the newest three papers stay on a reader.
        </p>
        <p>
          A paper per reader, each with its own sections, schedule, days and device, and a
          large-print layout for big type. Sign in to a paper you subscribe to and it prints in
          full as its own section. A Mac asleep at 5:30 builds the paper when it wakes. Stop the
          presses skips a morning. A source that fails prints “unavailable today” and the paper
          still comes out. The <a class="textlink" href={docs('docs/daily.md')} rel="noopener">Daily reference</a>
          has every setting.
        </p>
